{ Before starting, please view the study pinned comment. }
1. e4 (1. Nf3) 1... c6 { Officially the Caro Kann, your shield against 1 e4. } 2. d4 { This is the most common reply and so most of the chapters/variations will be based on it. } 2... d5 3. e5 { This is the advanced variation, and is the most popular AND testing line against the Caro, but I feel like this variation is better for black and that the game is real comfortable after this. The top level gms have won with black 3/4 times in this position. } 3... Bf5 { [%csl Rc8,Gf5][%cal Be7e6] } 4. h4 { A tricky try to cause black problems, and prevent e6, the solidifying move. } 4... h5 (4... e6 { [%cal Rg2g4,Rh4h5,Rf2f3] } 5. g4 Be4 6. f3 Bg6 7. h5 { Trapped bishops are bad!! } { [%csl Rg6,Rf5,Re4,Rd3][%cal Rc2d3,Rf3e4,Rg4f5,Rh5g6] }) (4... h6) 5. Bd3 Bxd3 6. Qxd3 { These are a few critical engine tries, so you have to be careful. Here, e6 is NOT the way to go. } 6... Qa5+ { This move has seen a lot of success for black. } 7. Nd2 { This move is a strategic success for black because the bishop is blocked, and can not utilize g5. } { [%csl Rd2,Gg5][%cal Rc1d2,Gc2c4,Gd2c4,Gd3c4] } (7. Bd2 Qa6 { [%cal Gd3a6,Gb8a6] } 8. e6 Qxd3 9. exf7+ Kxf7 10. cxd3 { If the queens are EVER traded, on d3 or a6, then black is really comfortable and the endgame is favorable for him because of all of white's weaknesses. And white doesn't have a proper way to avoid the trade, so the natural Bd2 is BAD. }) 7... e6 (7... Qa6 8. c4 { [%csl Ra6,Gc4][%cal Ra6c4,Gd3c4,Gd2c4] }) 8. Nf3 Nh6 { And now you just continue with natural development and try to grind down white's center into smithereens. The following is a game that just fizzles out into a draw. } { [%csl Bf5,Yd4,Yh4][%cal Gf5h4,Gf5d4] } 9. O-O (9. c3) 9... Nf5 { [%csl Yh4,Yd4][%cal Gf5d4,Gf5h4] } 10. Nb3 Qa6 11. Qd1 { This move now works because the King will no longer be stuck on d1 because it is already castled. } { [%csl Re1][%cal Ga6f1,Re1g1] } 11... Nd7 { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gd7c5,Gf8c5,Gc6c5] } 12. Bf4 Be7 { [%csl Yh4][%cal Gf5h4,Ge7h4] } 13. g3 { [%cal Gg3h4] } 13... Rc8 { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gc6c5,Gd7c5,Ge7c5] } 14. c3 c5 { A signature and powerful move in the advanced variation because it now strikes in the center. The rest of the game may prove to be instructive, but it is no longer about the opening, so I'll leave it without comment } { [%cal Gc5d4,Ge7a3,Ge7h4,Gc8c1,Bf5h4,Bf5d4,Ba6f1,Yd7b6,Yb6a4,Yb6c4] } 15. a4 Qb6 16. Ra2 a6 17. dxc5 Bxc5 18. Kg2 Nf8 19. a5 Qb5 20. Nxc5 Rxc5 21. Re1 Ng6 22. Be3 Rc8 23. Bb6 Nge7 24. Nd4 Qd7 25. Ra3 Rc4 26. Qf3 Qc8 27. Rd1 g6 28. b4 O-O 29. Bc5 Re8 30. Bxe7 Nxe7 31. Rb3 Nc6 32. Qe3 Qc7 33. Re1 Rc8 34. Qf4 Nxd4 35. cxd4 Rc3 36. Re3 Rc1 37. Rf3 Rf8 38. g4 hxg4 39. Qxg4 Qc4 40. Rfd3 Ra1 41. Qf4 Qc2 42. Qe3 Kg7 43. h5 Rh8 44. Qf4 Qe2 45. Qf6+ Kg8 46. Qd8+ Kg7 47. Qf6+ Kg8 48. Qd8+ { Jones, G. - Xiong, Jeffery, 1/2-1/2, St Louis Summer A, 2019, https://lichess.org/peUnUhjc } 1/2-1/2


