1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. Nf3 { This is the most natural move, and it just sort of fizzles out into a game after natural development. I don't think I need to cover it, let alone give it a few WHOLE chapter, but I think the top move in the database does deserve a full chapter. } 4... e6 { Black really has no problems here, and gets a nice edge. } 5. Be2 c5 (5... Nd7 6. O-O Ne7) 6. O-O (6. Be3 { This prevents Nc6 indirectly, by putting pressure on c5. } 6... Qb6 (6... cxd4 7. Nxd4 Ne7) 7. Nc3 { If you want to accept that poisoned pawn, you have my best of luck, but it gets risky and complex so do your own homework on it. } 7... Nc6 { This next game is a nice example of what could happen in this variation. } 8. Na4 (8. O-O Qxb2 (8... c4) 9. Nb5 c4 10. Nc7+ Kd7 11. Nxa8 Bxc2 12. Qc1 Ba3 13. Ne1 Bf5 14. Qd1 Nge7 15. Bd3 (15. Rc1 Rxa8 16. g4 Be4 17. f3 Bg6 18. h4 h5 19. gxh5 Nf5) 15... Bxd3 16. Nxd3 Qc3 17. Nc5+ Bxc5 18. Rc1 (18. dxc5 Rxa8) 18... Qd3 19. Qxd3) 8... Qa5+ 9. c3 c4 10. O-O b5 11. Nc5 Bxc5 12. dxc5 Nge7 13. Nd4 a6 14. f4 Be4 15. Qd2 O-O 16. Bf3 Bxf3 17. Rxf3 b4 18. Rh3 bxc3 19. bxc3 Rab8 20. Qc2 h6 21. Nxc6 Nxc6 22. Rf1 Qa3 23. f5 Rb2 24. Qd1 Qxc3 25. Bc1 Qc2 26. Bxb2 Qxb2 27. f6 Qd4+ 28. Qxd4 Nxd4 29. fxg7 Kxg7 30. Rg3+ Kh7 31. Ra3 Nc6 32. Rxa6 Nxe5 33. Rxe6 fxe6 34. Rxf8 c3 35. Rf1 d4 36. a4 d3 37. Kf2 Ng4+ 38. Ke1 Ne3 39. Rf2 Nc2+ 40. Kd1 Nd4 41. c6 c2+ 42. Rxc2 dxc2+ 43. Kc1 Nxc6 44. Kxc2 Kg6 45. Kd3 Kf5 46. Ke3 e5 47. h3 e4 { Jakovenko, Dmitrij - Eljanov, Pavel, 0-1, RUS-chT 17th, 2010, https://lichess.org/iC2MM1Df }) 6... Nc6 { [%csl Gd4] } 7. c3 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gc3d4] } 7... cxd4 8. cxd4 Nge7 9. a3 Nc8 { [%cal Gc8b6,Gf8e7,Gc8a7,Gb6c4,Gb7b5] } 10. Nbd2 Be7 11. b3 { [%cal Gb3c4] } 11... a5 { [%cal Ga5b4,Gc6b4,Ge7b4] } 12. Bb2 N8a7 13. Re1 O-O 14. Nf1 Rc8 15. Ng3 Bg6 16. Qd2 Qb6 17. Bd1 Rc7 18. h4 h6 19. Re2 Rfc8 20. Qf4 Nb8 21. Re3 Nb5 22. Ne2 Nd7 23. Nh2 Rc6 24. Ng4 Qd8 25. Rh3 Qf8 26. a4 Na3 27. Bxa3 Bxa3 28. Rg3 h5 29. Ne3 Bb2 30. Ra2 Bc1 31. Qg5 Qb4 32. Nf4 Bxe3 33. fxe3 Rc1 34. Nxg6 Rxd1+ 35. Kh2 fxg6 36. Qxg6 Qe7 37. Rf2 Qxh4+ 38. Rh3 Qxf2 { Shirov, Alexei - Karpov, Anatoly, 0-1, Las Palmas, 1994, https://lichess.org/6IRFW4O6 } 0-1


