1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. Nc3 e6 5. g4 Bg6 6. Nge2 (6. h4 h5 7. g5 { White's kingside is on lockdown since black has control over f5, the only pawn break. } { [%csl Gg4,Gh3,Gf4,Ge4,Bf5] }) 6... c5 { White is striking with all his might on the kingside, so you RETALIATE on the queenside and center. } 7. h4 h5 8. Nf4 Bh7 9. Nxh5 cxd4 { The pawn is compensated by white's weak center } 10. Nb5 { Queen takes is fine, but Nb5 is considered to be an improvement. } 10... Nc6 11. Nxd4 Nge7 12. Rh3 { The rest of this is the middle game which is good to study. } 12... a6 13. Bg5 Qb6 14. Nxc6 Nxc6 15. Bd3 Bxd3 16. Rxd3 Qb4+ 17. c3 Qe4+ { [%cal Be4e5] } 18. Re3 Qg2 19. Rc1 Qg1+ { [%cal Ge1e2,Gg1g4,Gg4h5] } 20. Kd2 Qxf2+ 21. Re2 Qc5 22. Qa4 Rc8 23. Rf1 Rc7 24. Kc1 d4!! 25. Rd1 Rd7 26. Red2 Qxe5 27. cxd4 Qe4 28. Rc2 f6 29. Bd2 b5 30. Qxa6 Nxd4 31. Rc8+ Kf7 32. Ng3 Qxg4 33. Rg1 Ne2+ 34. Nxe2 Qxe2 35. Rd1 Bb4 36. Qc6 Bxd2+ 37. Kc2 Ba5+ 38. Kb3 Qxd1+ { Grischuk, Alexander - Bareev, Evgeny, 0-1, Corus, 2003, https://lichess.org/GaodV30w } { We now have completed our repertoire against the ADVANCED VARIATION, and shall continue to learn more variations. } 39. Qc2 Rd3+ 40. Rc3 Qg4 41. a3 Qc4# { That's a smothered mate without a knight. } 0-1


