1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 { h5 is not the go-to move here since the Bishop is on g6, so fighting for control over g4 is pointless. } 6... h6 7. Nf3 (7. Nh3 { Honorable mention to this move as white scores quite well with it. } { [%cal Gh3f4,Gf4g6] } 7... Nf6 8. Nf4 Bh7 9. Bc4 e6 { Solid control over d5 white having an excellent bishop on h7 keeps black's position solid, and potential plans are there such as on the screen. Basically, you stay solid while improving your pieces, and if you want, utilizing the d file or maybe preparing a c5 push to weaken white's center. } { [%csl Gd5,Rd4][%cal Yb8d7,Yd7b6,Yb6d5,Yf6d5,Yd8d5,Yc6d5,Ye6d5,Bh7b1,Gf8e7,Ge8g8,Bd8d1,Rc6c5] }) 7... Nd7 { [%cal Gf3e5,Gd7e5] } 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2 Ngf6 { As you can see, d4 can be a potential weakness, and that by preventing white's only tricky tries, Ne5 and Sacs on g6, white really has nothing, and that black is solid and can find play on the weak square d5, weak pawn e4, and semi-open d file. } { [%csl Rd4,Gd5][%cal Gd7b6,Gc6c5] } 12. O-O-O { Middlegame } 12... Be7 13. Ne4 O-O 14. Kb1 c5 15. dxc5 Qc7 16. Nxf6+ Nxf6 17. Qe2 Qxc5 18. Ne5 Rac8 19. Bc1 Rfd8 20. g4 Rxd1 21. Rxd1 Bd6 22. Nd3 Qb5 23. f3 Nd5 24. Bd2 a5 25. Nc1 Qb6 26. c4 Qa6 27. b3 a4 28. Qe4 Nf6 29. Qe2 axb3 30. axb3 b5 31. g5 hxg5 32. Bxg5 bxc4 33. Bxf6 gxf6 34. Rd2 Bf4 35. Rc2 Bxc1 36. Rxc1 Ra8 37. Qb2 cxb3 38. Rd1 Kh7 39. Qxb3 Qa1+ 40. Kc2 Rc8+ 41. Kd3 Qxd1+ { Smirin, Ilia - Bareev, Evgeny, 0-1, FIDE GP, 2002, https://lichess.org/m3IBZzcv } 42. Qxd1 Rd8+ 43. Ke2 Rxd1 44. Kxd1 { [%cal Gh7h6,Gh6h5] } 0-1


