1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. Bd3 { This is a Bobby Fischer variation. Each of white's choices pose a specific problem to black, and you need to solve it. } 4... Nc6 5. c3 (5. Nf3 { White's play is wasted after } 5... Bg4 { The bishop is now developed } { [%csl Gg4][%cal Rd1f3] } 6. c3 Qc7) 5... Nf6 (5... Qc7 { This is also an option.... } { [%csl Rc1] }) 6. h3 (6. Bf4 Bg4 7. Qb3 Qc8 8. Nd2 e6 { White gets a bit more active play, but black is solid } { [%csl Gc4][%cal Gf8e7,Ge8g8,Gc6a5,Ga5c4,Ga8c8,Gc8c4] }) (6. Nf3 Bg4 { [%cal Gg4d1] }) 6... g6 7. Nf3 Bg7 8. O-O Bf5! { It seems as though white can just capture, and the pawns in front of your king are doubled. Well, this is inevitable,but } 9. Bxf5 (9. Bf4 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 O-O { Black has no more issues, and white lost is major attempt to create imbalances. Black has chances now, expect a minority attack. https://www.expert-chess-strategies.com/minority-attack.html
This is some random link I found that might explain what minority attack is. } { [%csl Gc4,Ge4,Rb2][%cal Ga8c8,Gc8c4,Ga7a6,Gb7b5,Gb5b4] }) 9... gxf5 { Through the lichess database (please have it on, click the book), it seems black has done the best in these lines. Obviously the kingside is shaky, but e5 more than compensates. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Bd5e4,Bf5e4,Bf6e4] } 10. Bf4 O-O 11. Ne5 e6 12. Nd2 Ne7 { The following is a game by Ian Rogers, it will serve as extra instruction, so you view it and learn the MIDDLEGAME plans. } 13. Re1 Ng6 14. Bh2 Ne4 15. Nd3 Bh6 16. Nf3 f6 17. Re2 b6 18. Qb3 Rc8 19. Kh1 Qd7 20. Rae1 Kh8 21. Ng1 Rfe8 22. f3 Nd2 23. Qd1 Nc4 24. f4 Nd6 25. Nf3 Ne4 26. g4 fxg4 27. hxg4 Bg7 28. Rg2 Rf8 29. Reg1 a5 30. Qe1 Qb5 31. Nf2 f5 32. gxf5 exf5 33. Nxe4 dxe4 34. Ng5 Rf6 35. Qd1 Bh6 36. Nh3 Nh4 37. Re2 Qd5 38. Re3 Nf3 39. Rg2 Rg6 40. Rxg6 hxg6 41. a3 Kg7 42. Bg3 b5 43. Ng1 Ne5 44. Qf1 Rh8 45. Kg2 Ng4 46. Re1 Rc8 47. Qe2 Kg8 48. Nh3 Bg7 49. Ng5 Bf6 50. Bh4 Rc7 51. Kg3 Bxg5 52. Bxg5 Rh7 53. a4 e3 54. Bh4 g5 55. fxg5 Qd6+ 56. Kh3 Nf2+ 57. Kg2 Rxh4 58. Qxe3 Qh2+ 59. Kf1 Ne4 { Nun, Jiri - Rogers, Ian, 0-1, CZE-chT 9495, 1995, https://lichess.org/nGSVXeml } 0-1


