1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. c4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Nf3 { And this is an invitation to an endgame, and the forcing line I mentioned in the previous chapter. } (6. Bg5 Be6) 6... Bg4 (6... g6 { This move avoids the endgame line. But I won't cover this... You can do your own digging on this, but you should take the forcing variation into consideration against a weaker opponent who might not know this, or if you just want a draw. }) 7. cxd5 Nxd5 8. Qb3 { After Qb3, the forcing line begins (actually, Qb3 is the best move because it keeps white's position active. Other tries give black too much so the line actually starts at 7cxd5. } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Yb3d5,Yc3d5] } 8... Bxf3 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gf3d5] } 9. gxf3 e6 10. Qxb7 Nxd4 11. Bb5+ Nxb5 12. Qc6+ Ke7 (12... Qd7 13. Qxa8+ { Whoops, that would be embarrassing. }) 13. Qxb5 Qd7 14. Nxd5+ Qxd5 15. Qxd5 exd5 { 8 forced moves, you better remember this properly. But it is pretty logical.
1. He attacks your knight, you take his and defend yours. And then you defend yours again.
2. He takes your pawn, you take his. He checks you with his bishop, you take it.
3. He doesn't take your knight and checks you, you move the king. Everything else is basic. } 16. Be3 (16. Bf4 Ke6 { You respond to everything (well not everything, but most of white's tries) with Ke6. }) (16. O-O Ke6 17. Re1+ Kf5 { Nothing scary. } { [%cal Gf3f4,Gf7f6] }) 16... Ke6 { The following game fizzles out into a draw. Basically:
You place your king on an active square like e6.
You place your bishop on b4 to guard e1.
And you protect d5 and develop your rooks. } 17. O-O-O Bb4 18. Rd3 Rhc8+ 19. Kb1 Bc5 20. Rc1 Bxe3 21. fxe3 g5 22. Ra3 Rxc1+ 23. Kxc1 a5 24. Kd1 Rb8 25. b3 Rb5 26. Ra4 h5 27. Ke1 f5 28. h3 Kd6 29. Ke2 Rc5 30. Kd2 Rb5 31. Ke2 Rc5 32. Kd2 Rb5 { Ivanchuk, V. - Najer, E., 1/2-1/2, TCh-TUR Super League 2017, https://lichess.org/BIJJfPm7 } 1/2-1/2


