1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. c4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Bg5 dxc4 7. Bxc4 (7. d5 Ne5 8. Bxc4 Nxc4 9. Qa4+ Bd7 10. Qxc4 h6 11. Bh4 g5 12. Bg3 Bg7 { A solid and advanced set up with a weakness on d5, black should be quite happy with the opening. } { [%csl Rd5,Gg7,Gh6,Gg5,Gf6][%cal Ge8h8,Bc8c1,Bd8d1] }) 7... h6 { This move before accepting the pawn. } 8. Bh4 Qxd4 9. Qxd4 Nxd4 10. O-O-O { Now be wary of checkmate ideas as illustrated on the screen. } { [%cal Yd1d8,Yc3b5,Yb5c7,Rd4c6] } 10... e5 11. f4 (11. Nf3 Nxf3 12. Bxf6 gxf6 13. Bb5+ Ke7 14. Nd5+ Kd8 { Recapturing the black knight is now forced or white will be down a piece. } 15. gxf3 Bd6 16. Nxf6 (16. Rd2 Be6 17. Rhd1 Bc5 18. Nb6+ Ke7 19. Nxa8 Rxa8 { The Bishops compensate for the exchange. }) 16... Ke7 17. Nd5+ Kf8) 11... Bg4 { We are now planning Rc8 with that attack on the pieces, and the pin. White has to prevent this so... } { [%csl Gd1][%cal Gg4d1] } 12. Nf3 Bxf3 13. gxf3 Rc8 14. fxe5 (14. Bf1 Nd5 { Utilizing the pin and getting out of any attack that may arise from fxe5. } { [%cal Gd5c3] } 15. fxe5 Nxc3 16. bxc3 { The following is an example continuation of how the game might turn out. } 16... Ba3+ 17. Kb1 Nxf3 18. Bh3 { Stay on the back rank! } { [%csl Gd8][%cal Gh4d8,Gd1d8] } 18... Ra8 19. Bd7+ Kf8 20. Bg3 Ng5 21. e6) 14... Rxc4 15. exf6 Ne6 *


