1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 Nf6 { Obviously, you can transpose into other Caro like positions by playing Bg4, and if you're looking for a quick way to play this opening without studying too much, you can play the slightly weaker Bg4 for the sake of time. The major reason these moves are slightly weaker is because the white d pawn is not yet committed to anything. } 4. e5 { Only critical try, sidelines are simple. } (4. Qe2 d4) 4... Ne4 { View the sidelines and one partially critical line as well. Knowing all the sidelines instead of the mainline will help you more so check those out (the big gray box with all the lines). } 5. Ne2 { Time is of the essence, if black goes for something slow, then the knight on e4 could be proven to be a bit misplaced. Fortunately for us, the knight is a GOOD piece (my favorite after the queen and pawn), and we must make use of the fact that it hits f2. } { [%csl Ye4] } (5. d4 Qa5 6. Bd3 (6. Bd2 Nxd2 7. Qxd2 Bf5 8. Nh4 Bd7 9. Bd3 e6 10. Nf3 c5 { And you have a game here, view the game in the database or caleb's video for more info. }) 6... Bg4 { [%cal Ga5e1,Gg4d1,Gd3e4,Gd5e4] } 7. O-O Nxc3 8. bxc3 e6 9. Bd2) (5. Nxe4 dxe4 6. Ng5 Qd5 { [%cal Gd5e5,Gd2d4,Ge4d3] } 7. Qe2 Bf5 8. g4 Bg6 9. Bg2 Qxe5 10. Nxe4 h5 11. g5 { And, black is clearly NOT worse, I will have you know that Bxe4 is fine, but this stuff is now an interesting game, not knowledge of theory/openings. } { [%csl Gg4,Gf5,Gh3] }) (5. Qe2 Nxc3 6. dxc3 Bg4 7. h3 Bxf3 8. Qxf3 e6 { The theme: White goes for a kingside attack (this is the most consistent theme in the Caro), black develops and utilizes the queenside space he has. }) (5. h3 { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gh3g4] } 5... e6 { White wasted a tempo to keep our bishop locked in, so we just do our stuff and try to utilize the tempo. } 6. Qe2 Nxc3 7. dxc3 c5 { And this is similar to previous sidelines. }) (5. d3 Nxc3 6. bxc3 Bg4 7. h3 Bxf3 8. Qxf3 e6 9. d4 { And, I feel like going forward is pointless, if you paid good attention to previous lines, you should know the ideas and plans and this variation is nothing special here. }) (5. Be2 Nxc3 6. dxc3 Bg4 7. Nd4 Bxe2 8. Qxe2 e6 9. O-O { It's going to turn into a game. }) 5... Qb6!! { Mate on f2, this forces white to finally commit the pawn to d4. } { [%csl Gf2][%cal Bb6f2,Be4f2] } (5... e6 6. d3 Nc5 7. d4 Ncd7 { And you'd reach this french like structure, down a few tempi, which is NOT good. Note if you head to e4, you reach the same position, but now white got d4 in, therefore you lost a tempo. }) 6. d4 { d3 is no longer an option. } 6... e6 (6... Bg4 7. Nfg1 { Quite awkward. } { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gf2f3] }) 7. Ng3 (7. Nfg1 f6 { We are giving a square for our knight on g5, AND, threatening to take on e5 because the d pawn is pinned so white cannot do something like h4. } 8. f3 Ng5 9. exf6 gxf6 { Note taking on g5 weakens b2. } 10. f4 Ne4 11. Ng3 Bd7 12. Nxe4 dxe4 13. c3 { [%cal Gc6c5,Gb8a6] } 13... c5 14. d5 exd5 15. Qxd5 Nc6 16. Qh5+ (16. Qxe4+ Ne7 { [%cal Ge8c8] }) 16... Kd8 { [%csl Gd7,Gc6,Gb6,Gf8,Rh1,Rg1,Rf1,Re1,Rc1,Ra1][%cal Gd8c7,Ga8d8] }) 7... c5 8. Bd3 (8. Nxe4 dxe4 9. Ng5 cxd4) 8... Nxg3 9. hxg3 cxd4 { [%csl Gh7,Ge7,Gc5,Gc6,Gd7][%cal Gd3h7,Gh1h7,Gb8c6,Gc8d7,Gf8e7,Ge7c5] } 10. Ng5 (10. a3 { [%csl Gb4][%cal Ga3b4,Gb2b4] } 10... Nc6 11. b4 Bd7 12. Bf4 h6 { [%cal Gg7g5] } 13. g4 O-O-O { [%cal Rg4g5,Rh6g5,Yh1h8,Yf8b4] } 14. O-O { [%cal Gg7g5,Gh6h5] } (14. g5 hxg5 15. Rxh8 Bxb4+)) (10. Nxd4 { [%cal Rb6d4,Gd3b5,Bd1d4] } 10... Bc5 11. c3 Nc6 12. Nxc6 (12. Be3 Bd7 { White is falling. }) 12... Bxf2+ 13. Kf1 bxc6 { The white king is a clear weakness, white's development is not the best. }) 10... Nc6 11. Nxh7 Bd7 12. Nf6+ gxf6 13. Rxh8 O-O-O { [%cal Gf8b4,Rd8h8] } 14. Kf1 (14. Qh5 { Another nice game follows. } 14... fxe5 15. Bg5 Qxb2 16. Rd1 Bb4+ 17. Kf1 Rxh8 18. Qxh8+ Kc7 19. c3 dxc3 20. Rb1 Qa3 21. Rb3 Qa5 22. Qg7 b6 23. a3 Bxa3 24. Qxf7 Bb2 25. Kg1 Qa1+ 26. Kh2 Qd1 27. Rxb2 cxb2 28. Qh7 e4 29. Bf4+ e5 { Ponkratov, Pavel - Shimanov, Alexandr, 0-1, Khanty-Mansiysk Governor Cup 8th, 2012, https://lichess.org/YTaTWa6d } { The power of an impenetrable center. }) 14... fxe5!! $17 $44 { NO REVERSE!! One of the greatest centers in chess history. } { [%csl Gf7,Ge5,Gd5,Gd4,Ge6][%cal Ge5e4,Gf7f5,Ge6e5] } 15. Qh5 { The following is a game by 2020 Candidate, Ding Liren, played in 2013. } 15... e4 16. Be2 Bd6 17. a3 e3 18. Rxd8+ Nxd8 19. Qf3 exf2 20. Qxf2 Be8 21. b4 Qc7 22. Bb2 Nc6 23. b5 Ne7 24. g4 e5 25. Rc1 Bc5 26. Qf6 Ng6 27. c3 d3 28. Qf5+ Bd7 29. Qxd3 e4 30. Qxd5 Qf4+ 31. Bf3 Qe3 { Fang, Yan - Ding, Liren, 0-1, CHN-chT, 2013, https://lichess.org/ZfLSEyZn } 0-1


[Event "My Repertoire Against e4: The Caro Kann Defense: Fantasy/Tartakower Variation"]
[Date "2020.08.06"]
[White "Oleksienko Mikhailo"]
[Black "Samuel Shankland"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B12"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Maróczy Variation"]
[StudyName "My Repertoire Against e4: The Caro Kann Defense"]
[ChapterName "Fantasy/Tartakower Variation"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yteYURRC/mZ8Q1RcW"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Flyingjat"]
[UTCDate "2020.08.06"]
[UTCTime "21:19:18"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. f3 { This sort of move is counter intuitive, and will rarely occur in your games, however, because of the fact that it has some critical lines, I suggest powerful players who may face preparation view this. } 3... dxe4 (3... e6 4. Nc3 Bb4 5. Bd2 Ne7 6. Bd3 O-O { I like this line too... if you're like me and is too lazy to learn all the lines, then consider this as your choice against the Fantasy Variation! }) 4. fxe4 { Two central pawns, has the Caro-Kann been refuted? } 4... e5 { This is probably one of the only moves that does best to survive this weird strategy. We have to strike in the center, and lay claims to it. This move's goal is to neutralize white's central advantage. } 5. Nf3 (5. dxe5 Qh4+ { A neat tactic, and the other threat is hitting d4. So Nc3 would lose to exd4. Basically, Nf3 is the only move that survives BOTH threats. An unaware opponent might do something weird, and grant you the point(s). } { [%cal Gh4e4] }) 5... Bg4 6. Bc4 (6. c3 { The following is another tricky variation that you might face as preparation. } 6... Nd7 7. Bd3 { This move is basically signaling that white wants to bust open the h7-b1 diagonal, how are you going to deal with it? } 7... Bd6 8. O-O Ngf6 9. a4 { A move that prevents b5, and at first it may seem unclear why white would do such a thing. The answer is to maneuver the knight to c4, and tear down e5. This falls with white's plan of tearing down e5 and opening the diagonal. } { [%csl Ye5][%cal Yb1d2,Yd2c4,Yc4e5] } 9... O-O 10. Nbd2 Re8 11. Nc4 { [%csl Ge5,Gd6][%cal Gc4d6,Gc4e5] } 11... Bc7 { The black queen looks awkward. } 12. Qc2 { Popilski, G. - Nakamura, Hi, 1/2-1/2, Millionaire Chess Op 2015, https://lichess.org/V2U577ui } { View the game that I have given a link to, the only game in opening explorer. We have been following it now, for quite some moves. If you're curious about this line, view Caleb's video. }) 6... Nd7 { Of course, the white player also has chances and so if you do something peculiar, you're going to get crushed by a tactic. } (6... Nf6 7. Bxf7+ Kxf7 8. Nxe5+ Kg8 9. Nxg4) 7. O-O (7. c3 { This move prepares the sneaky Qb3, guaranteeing the loss of a pawn. So you have to respond to it properly. } 7... Bh5 { But, at the end of the day, it's just a different move order and you should reach the positions from our mainline. } (7... b5)) 7... Ngf6 8. c3 Bd6 { You have to hold the strongpoint on e5. } { [%csl Ge5] } 9. Bg5 (9. Qb3 O-O 10. Qxb7 Rb8 11. Qxc6 Bxf3 12. Rxf3 Rb6 13. Qa4 exd4 14. cxd4 Rb4 { The b7 pawn isn't worth these complications for white has he/she abandons all their chances at a advantage. Plus, white's center is falling after this so you should be content if this happens. } 15. Qc2 Qb6 { [%csl Re4,Rd4][%cal Bf6e4,Bb6d4,Bd4g1] }) 9... O-O 10. Nbd2 Qc7 { [%csl Ye5][%cal Gd6e5,Bd7e5,Bc7e5] } 11. Qe1 { [%cal Ge1h4] } 11... Bh5 12. Qh4 Bg6 { I've inputted a nice game that goes with the theme of this variation, and as always, Caleb's video is available. } 13. Kh1 { Getting the king out of nasty Qb6+ ideas (obviously you have to be on a lookout for these as black). } 13... Rae8 { [%cal Ge5d4,Gc3d4,Gf6e4,Gg6e4,Ge8e4] } 14. Rae1 b5 15. Bb3 { As I've stressed on in the chapter pinned comment, this seldom occurs, but if you miraculously get into such a position, you should know that white's major plan is to tear down e5, and he does with setting up a battery on the f6 knight, to overwork the d7 one. That makes life uncomfortable for black. } 15... Nh5 (15... a6 16. dxe5 Bxe5 (16... Nxe5 17. Bxf6 gxf6 18. Nd4 { White is better here. } 18... Be7 19. Re2 Kh8 20. Nf5) 17. Nxe5 Qxe5 { Now, this position is practically equal, except for two things.
1. The e5 strongpoint has crumbled and white's pieces come to life for that.
2. The Bishop pair is lost, something I don't care about, but is something most players care about more than principles, so I do have to include it. }) (15... c5 { Nh5 is for players who prefer more complex positions, c5 leads to simpler stuff. } { [%csl Gb5][%cal Gc5c4,Ga7a6] } 16. dxe5 Nxe5 17. Bxf6 gxf6 18. Re3 { And now, we've reached this awesome position full of imbalances. } (18. Qxf6 Nd3 19. Re3 c4) 18... c4 { We have a slightly worse pawn structure, BUT we have a nice HOLE on d3 for our knight (something it can use to out perform rooks) and a lot of queenside play. } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Ya7a5,Yb5b4] }) 16. dxe5 { The idea of Nh5 is to make Bxf6 illegal, because there is no more knight on f6. Also, keep in mind c5 is usually a useful asset. Note if white ever inserted this exchange earlier, the semi-open e file will prove to be quite an asset for black, bearing down on that isolated e pawn. But of course, if white ever got in the move e5, then black will face problems. } 16... Nxe5 17. Nxe5 Bxe5 18. Nf3 Bf6 (18... a5 { Bishop pairs have to be conceded, but this concession is not that crucial because you get a lot of queenside play and that knight does quite a nice job of controlling f4, limiting white's attacking potential. Also, you can target the weakness on e5. } 19. Nxe5 Rxe5 20. Bd1 h6 21. Bc1 Rfe8 { [%cal Ge8e4] }) 19. Bxf6 Nxf6 20. e5 { That is an edge for white, but we get the d5 square. } 20... Nd5 21. Qd4 Rd8 (21... c5 { A tricky move Sam Shankland missed. } 22. Qxd5 Rd8 { [%csl Rc6,Rb7,Ra8,Re4,Rc4,Rd1,Rd2,Rd3,Rd4,Rd6,Rd7,Rd8,Gd5,Re6][%cal Rd8d1,Rg6e4,Rb5c4,Rc7c6,Rc7b7,Rd8a8,Rf7e6] }) 22. Qc5 Qb6 23. Qxb6 axb6 24. Nd4 c5 25. Nc6 c4 26. Nxd8 cxb3 27. axb3 Rxd8 28. Rd1 Bc2 29. c4 Bxd1 30. Rxd1 bxc4 31. bxc4 Ra8 32. cxd5 Kf8 33. Kg1 Ke7 34. Kf2 f6 35. e6 Kd6 36. Ke3 g6 37. b3 Rc8 38. g4 Rc3+ 39. Kd4 Rc5 40. Ke4 Rc2 41. h3 Re2+ 42. Kd3 Re5 43. Kc4 f5 44. gxf5 gxf5 45. Kb5 Re3 46. Ka6 f4 47. b4 Rxh3 48. Kxb6 Re3 49. b5 f3 50. Ka7 f2 51. b6 h5 52. b7 Ra3+ 53. Kb8 Rf3 54. Ka8 Ra3+ 55. Kb8 Rf3 56. Rf1 h4 57. Ka7 Ra3+ 58. Kb6 Rb3+ 59. Ka6 Ra3+ 60. Kb6 Rb3+ 61. Ka6 Ra3+ 62. Kb5 Rb3+ 63. Kc4 Rxb7 64. Rxf2 h3 65. Rh2 { Oleksienko, Mikhailo - Shankland, Samuel L, 1/2-1/2, Dresden ZMDI op 22nd, 2013, https://lichess.org/Jw6uI0ua } 1/2-1/2


