1. e4 c6 2. d3 (2. c4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. cxd5 Nf6 5. Nc3 (5. Qa4+ Nbd7 6. Nc3 g6 7. Nf3 Bg7 { [%csl Rd5] } 8. Bc4 O-O { And you'll regain the weak pawn soon enough. } 9. d3 a6 10. Qa3 b6 { [%csl Gd5,Bc8][%cal Yc8b7,Yb7d5,Yf6d5] }) 5... Nxd5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Bb5 { Obviously, you could transpose into the Panov earlier, but after Bb5, you cannot. This means we have to play e6, blocking our Bishop, but white's Bishop is also on a ''sub-optimal square'' like NM Caleb Denby phrased it. } 7... e6) (2. Bc4 d5 3. Bb3 dxe4 4. Qh5 { And there's Mate_on_f7. Please don't get Scholared on move 5. } { [%csl Rf7][%cal Bb3f7,Bh5f7] } 4... e6 5. Nc3 Nf6 { Now, I'm pretty sure such a position doesn't need to be analyzed, it's like playing against an offbeat opening, you play chess, not opening prep. }) 2... d5 3. Nd2 (3. Nf3 { g6 is an option or } { [%cal Gg7g6] } 3... dxe4 4. dxe4 Qxd1+ 5. Kxd1 { Absolute equality... }) 3... g6 4. Ngf3 Bg7 5. g3 e5 6. Bg2 Ne7 { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gg7e5] } 7. O-O O-O 8. b4 { A game the famous Teimour Radjabov lost. } { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gb4c5] } (8. Re1 { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge4d5,Ge1e5,Gf3e5] } 8... d4 9. a4 { This is now a reversed Kings Indian Defense (KID), or the King's Indian Attack (KIA). } (9. Nc4 b5 10. Ncxe5 f6) 9... c5 { [%cal Gc5d4,Ge5d4] } 10. Nc4 Nbc6 { White has never won in the 11 games this position has been reached, so you can continue to play the King's Indian with reversed colors, I don't want to dwell more on sidelines, as you should be fine, view Caleb's video for a LOT more info on this variation. }) 8... a5 9. bxa5 (9. c3 axb4 10. cxb4 Na6 { [%csl Rb4,Rc3,Re3][%cal Gd5d4,Gc6c5,Ba8a1,Ya6b4] }) 9... Qxa5 { White gets a lot of rapid development. } 10. Bb2 d4 11. Nb3 Qc7 { White aims to tear down the center by challenging the head of it. } 12. c3 dxc3 { One brilliant move refutes the other. } 13. Bxc3 { [%csl Gb7,Gc6,Rd3,Ra2] } 13... c5 14. Nfd2 b5 15. Qc2 Na6 16. Rfc1 Be6 17. Nf1 Nc6 18. Ne3 c4 19. dxc4 bxc4 20. Nd5 cxb3 21. Nxc7 bxc2 22. Nxa8 Nd4 23. Nb6 Rb8 24. Ba5 Bh6 25. Bf1 Bxc1 26. Rxc1 Nc6 27. Rxc2 Nxa5 28. Nd5 Nb4 29. Rc5 Nbc6 30. a4 Kg7 31. Kg2 Rb7 32. h4 Ra7 33. Nc3 Bd7 34. Bb5 Nb3 35. Rd5 Nbd4 36. f4 exf4 37. gxf4 Nxb5 38. axb5 Ne7 39. Rc5 Rb7 40. h5 Nc8 41. hxg6 hxg6 42. Kg3 Na7 43. f5 Nxb5 { Radjabov, Teimour - Cheparinov, Ivan, 0-1, Baku FIDE GP, 2008, https://lichess.org/4tIVRq9b } *


